libhsakmt (fmm): never drop the CPU mapping of a released host-VA range
(gfx803, ROCm 10.0)

NOT APPLIED as of 2026-09-07. scripts/build/rocr-clr.sh no longer includes
this patch in its apply list. Kept in the tree, unwired, for the reasons
below.

WHY THIS WAS PULLED
--------------------
A ComfyUI user (RX 580 2048SP, 8 GB) reported VRAM that PyTorch's own
allocator had released was not returned to the driver's free pool until
the whole process exited: loading one large checkpoint, then a second
different checkpoint, then explicitly asking ComfyUI to free everything
(POST /free with unload_models and free_memory) left the amdgpu driver's
own counter (mem_info_vram_used) unmoved even though PyTorch's tracked
pool (torch_vram_total) dropped to near zero. That is exactly what this
patch's FIX section promises: "The VA range remains mapped ... until
process exit." Reproduced on the project's own gfx803 box (2026-09-07):
loading Anima (a Cosmos-Predict2 video model) then a stock SD1.5
checkpoint through ComfyUI, then POSTing /free, left mem_info_vram_used
at 8.02 GB out of 8 GB total. With this one patch reversed and nothing
else changed, the identical sequence ended at 2.94 GB. The gap is not
explained by va-reuse-defer's FIFO (FMM_REUSE_QUEUE_MAX=256 entries /
FMM_REUSE_BYTES_MAX=1GiB, present on both the 7.14 and 10.0 lines
identically) -- this patch is the one line, 10.0-only, that removes a
release path entirely rather than bounding it.

Before pulling the patch, its own original crash was re-verified absent
on the current stack, using the same three methods its own VERIFIED
section names: a raw torch churn loop (safetensors.get_tensor() + .to
("cuda", non_blocking=True) with no sync between transfers, run 200
iterations over all 1145 tensors of v1-5-pruned-emaonly.safetensors --
the original claim was "~2 faults per 25s pass, 100% of runs", so 200
clean iterations is roughly 8x that threshold with zero
VM_CONTEXT1_PROTECTION_FAULT hits in dmesg); 5x ComfyUI checkpoint
load/unload cycles (POST /prompt loading the SD1.5 checkpoint, POST /free,
repeated 5 times); and one full sd1.5 txt2img generation (6 steps,
KSampler -> VAEDecode -> SaveImage) that completed successfully end to
end. All three passed clean on 2026-09-07, kernel 7.1.8, with every other
gfx803 patch on the 10.0 line still applied. This does not prove the
original race is gone for good -- kernel and amdgpu driver updates since
the patch was written could have closed the specific window it worked
around, or the repro could need a condition this session didn't hit. It
does mean the patch's crash could not be reproduced against the current
pinned source, while its cost (unbounded VRAM retention) reproduced
immediately and consistently.

HOW TO RE-VERIFY OR REWRITE THIS
---------------------------------
To check whether the original crash is back: run the raw torch churn loop
above (a minimal script is not committed; rebuild it from the WHY section's
description -- open any sd1.5-class safetensors checkpoint, get_tensor()
every key to CPU once, then loop calling .to("cuda", non_blocking=True) on
each tensor with no synchronize() between transfers, for at least 200
iterations) against a build with this patch NOT applied, and watch dmesg
for VM_CONTEXT1_PROTECTION_FAULT / "Failed to quiesce KFD" / GPU resets.
To check the VRAM-release regression this patch causes if reapplied: load
two different large checkpoints back to back through ComfyUI, POST /free,
and compare /sys/class/drm/card*/device/mem_info_vram_used before and
after -- with the patch applied it will not drop; without it, it should.

If the crash reproduces again on some future kernel/driver/GPU
combination, the fix is not to reapply this patch as-is. The correct
shape is narrower: track, per released host-VA range, whether a KFD
userptr registration (hsaKmtRegisterMemory* / hsa_amd_memory_lock) is
still outstanding at release time. When it is not, do the original
munmap()+mmap(PROT_NONE) immediately, exactly as upstream and exactly as
this patch currently forbids unconditionally -- that is the common case
and it is what returns VRAM promptly. When a registration is still
outstanding, defer the real munmap()+reprotect through the same bounded
FIFO va-reuse-defer.patch already added to this same file
(FMM_REUSE_QUEUE_MAX / FMM_REUSE_BYTES_MAX, drained at the same GPU-busy
flush points), with the real teardown firing either when
hsaKmtDeregisterMemoryCtx explicitly clears the lock for that VA or when
the entry ages out of the FIFO, whichever comes first. That keeps the
crash fixed with the same bounded worst case (about 1 GB held past its
natural free point) the codebase already relies on elsewhere, instead of
an unbounded hold for the life of the process. Any rewrite along these
lines must re-pass both verification methods above -- the crash repro AND
the VRAM-release check -- before it ships.

ORIGINAL PATCH BELOW
---------------------

WHY
---
`reserved_aperture_release()` in projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c
munmaps a released CPU-accessible host-VA range and re-reserves it as
PROT_NONE "to release shmem pages for MAP_SHARED allocations". Releasing
the *userspace* handle is not the end of that range's life: the kernel
keeps the KFD userptr BO (created by `hsa_amd_memory_lock`, e.g. PyTorch's
H2D staging windows) mapped in GPUVM until it is explicitly destroyed.
Any GPU work still queued behind the unmap reads a VA whose PTEs the
eviction path has already torn down -> VM_CONTEXT1_PROTECTION_FAULT on a
live buffer.

Reproduces deterministically on gfx803 loading ANY sd1.5-class checkpoint
through torch (`safetensors.get_tensor` + `.to("cuda")` in a loop; ~2
faults per 25 s pass, 100% of runs) and was the root cause of the
intermittent ComfyUI SIGSEGV/hang on the 10.0 line. The same race exists
on every architecture, but there the kernel recovers via userptr
eviction/restore (upstream's own comment in `__fmm_release` warns that
"munmap would cause an eviction"); on CIK the queues cannot be preempted
reliably, so one fault wedges the CP: "Queue preemption failed",
"Failed to quiesce KFD", GPU-reset cascades, and processes that hang at
`DQM create queue` (this is the gpu_recovery=0 stall captured end-to-end
on real Polaris hardware during this investigation).

Keeping the mapping is exactly what the deferred-release FIFO already
does for parked objects; the shmem-page reclaim it buys is not worth a
wedged GPU.

FIX
---
Drop the munmap()+mmap(PROT_NONE) pair from `reserved_aperture_release()`;
the mbind() NUMA-policy reset stays. The VA range remains mapped (and
userspace-reserved in fmm's vm tree) until process exit.

Verified on RX 580 (gfx803), kernel 7.1.12 Fedora stock (gpu_recovery=1):
before - 2 PROTECTION_FAULTs + resets on every repro run; after - 0 faults
and 0 resets over 4x torch-load repros, 5x ComfyUI checkpoint loads, and a
full sd1.5 txt2img generation (6 steps, EXIT=0).

Applies with: git apply (rocm-systems is cloned as a real git repo).

--- a/projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c
+++ b/projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c
@@ -707,24 +707,17 @@
 	}
 
 	if (app->is_cpu_accessible) {
-		void *mmap_ret;
-
 		/* Reset NUMA policy */
 		mbind(address, MemorySizeInBytes, MPOL_DEFAULT, NULL, 0, 0);
 
-		/*
-		 * Drop the CPU mapping to release shmem pages for MAP_SHARED
-		 * allocations, then re-reserve the VA range as PROT_NONE.
-		 * Callers hold fmm_mutex (see contract above), which serializes
-		 * this against aperture allocations, so no allocation can claim
-		 * the VA in the window between munmap() and mmap().
+		/* GFX803_FMM_KEEP_USERPTR_MAP: do NOT munmap here. A KFD
+		 * userptr (hsa_amd_memory_lock) keeps the kernel mapping of
+		 * this range alive after userspace released it, and queued
+		 * GPU work still reads it. Unmapping now evicts those PTEs
+		 * under the GPU; on gfx803 the resulting VM fault cannot be
+		 * recovered without a CP reset. Keep the mapping (the mbind
+		 * above already resets the NUMA policy).
 		 */
-		munmap(address, MemorySizeInBytes);
-		mmap_ret = mmap(address, MemorySizeInBytes, PROT_NONE,
-			MAP_ANONYMOUS | MAP_NORESERVE | MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_FIXED,
-			-1, 0);
-		if (mmap_ret == MAP_FAILED)
-			pr_err("Failed to reserve VA range %p: %s\n", address, strerror(errno));
 	}
 }
 
